<!-- BEGIN: RCA MAS instructions ============================================ -->

## RCA MAS — bug investigation tool

This repo bundles RCA MAS at [./rca-mas/](./rca-mas/). It is a multi-agent
bug investigator: given a bug report (a markdown file, pasted ticket text,
a stack trace, or a GitHub issue number), it scans this repo, diagnoses
the root cause, and proposes a fix as a unified diff.

### Trigger detection

Treat **any** of these as a request to run the MAS pipeline:

- The phrase "**rca-mas**" or "**RCA MAS**" anywhere in the user message
- "**use rca-mas to** {find / investigate / diagnose / resolve / fix / triage} ..."
- "**run rca-mas on** ..." or "**rca-mas:** ..."
- "**investigate {this bug | this ticket | this issue}** with rca-mas"

When triggered, follow the steps below. Do not ask for clarification on
trivial points (like which run flag) — pick the default and proceed.

### Step 1 — Identify the bug input

The bug input is one of:

**(a) A file path** the user named (e.g. `bug.md`, `tickets/PROJ-123.md`,
`c:/path/to/issue.md`). Use it as-is.

**(b) Inline ticket / bug text** in the user's message. This is the most
common form. It can look like any of:
   - A pasted JIRA / Linear / GitHub ticket with headings like
     "Steps to reproduce", "Expected", "Actual"
   - A raw stack trace plus a sentence of context
   - A free-form paragraph describing the bug
   - A QA Slack message pasted verbatim

  In **all** of these cases, save the user's text (the part of the message
  that describes the bug, **not** the trigger phrase like "use rca-mas to
  find the issue:") to `.rca-mas/bug.md`, creating the `.rca-mas/`
  directory if needed:

  ```bash
  mkdir -p .rca-mas
  # write the pasted bug text to .rca-mas/bug.md
  ```

  Then use `.rca-mas/bug.md` as the bug input path. If the pasted text
  looks thin (no stack trace, no file/function names, < 3 lines),
  proceed anyway but note in the final summary that the diagnosis may be
  weak — do not block on asking for more.

**(c) A GitHub issue number** (e.g. "issue 42", "#42", "github issue 42").
Use `--issue` mode instead of writing a file:
  ```bash
  bash ./rca-mas/rca-mas.sh --issue 42
  ```
  Add `--repo owner/name` only if the current repo is not the right
  GitHub remote.

**(d) Re-use the last bug input** if the user says "re-run rca-mas" or
"try rca-mas again". The previous `.rca-mas/bug.md` (or the last
explicit path) is the input.

### Step 2 — Run the pipeline

From the repo root, in **report-only** mode (the default — never edits
the working tree):

```bash
bash ./rca-mas/rca-mas.sh <bug-input-path>
```

Wall time is typically 3–8 minutes on a small/medium repo.

### Step 3 — Narrate progress

While the pipeline runs, tail the structured event log so the user can
see stage transitions:

```bash
tail -f .rca-mas/runs/latest/log.jsonl
```

You may translate the JSONL events into one-line status updates
("Briefing complete · 141 files, tier S", "Agent 1a investigating",
"Diagnosis written · confidence 0.92", etc.).

### Step 4 — Show the outputs

When the pipeline finishes, show the user:

1. The full report:
   ```bash
   cat .rca-mas/runs/latest/report.md
   ```
2. The proposed patch (only if `report.md` shows status `FIX`):
   ```bash
   cat .rca-mas/runs/latest/patches/fix.diff
   ```
3. A one-sentence summary in your own words: root cause, confidence,
   what the next action is.

### Step 5 — Hand back to the human

Do **not** apply the patch. Print the two commands the user can run
themselves if they want to apply it:

```bash
git apply --check .rca-mas/runs/latest/patches/fix.diff   # dry-run
git apply .rca-mas/runs/latest/patches/fix.diff           # apply
```

### Variations

- "**resolve / fix the bug**" still means report-only. Only add
  `--validate` (which applies the patch in an isolated worktree and runs
  tests) if the user explicitly says "validate", "sandbox", "run the
  tests", or "apply in isolation".
- "**show me the last rca-mas report**" → `cat .rca-mas/runs/latest/report.md`
  without re-running.
- "**what would rca-mas suggest for ...**" → still run the full pipeline;
  there is no preview mode.

### Things to never do

- Never edit files under `./rca-mas/` — that's the tool itself.
- Never investigate `./rca-mas/**` or `./.rca-mas/**` as the bug source.
  They are tool code and run artifacts.
- Never commit, push, tag, or open a PR with the patch unless the user
  explicitly asks. The patch is always for human review.
- Never invent a bug file path the user did not give you. If the input
  is ambiguous, default to writing the pasted text to `.rca-mas/bug.md`
  rather than guessing.

<!-- END: RCA MAS instructions ============================================== -->
